WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.497 --> 00:00:03.497
(audience cheering)

00:00:05.352 --> 00:00:06.870
- That was... Now, I know
from our last interview

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.090
that you're a great singer,
but that is awesome.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.320
I loved your whole song.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.120
- Well, you know, desperate times, right?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.940
- No, those are just regular great times.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.970
- Well, the show's been
on the air 20 years.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.803
- Geez.
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:20.172
- And so...

00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:22.042
(audience cheering)

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:22.875
- I did not realize.

00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:23.708
- That's the year that you won.

00:00:23.708 --> 00:00:24.541
- I was gonna say 20 years.

00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:26.338
You are a fan.

00:00:26.338 --> 00:00:28.742
(laughing)

00:00:28.742 --> 00:00:32.160
- 'Cause I used to wear this
pink ring every time you sang,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.420
and I was pretty sure-

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.980
- It was you.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.440
20 years, though. Longevity
is hard in this career.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.400
- It's super weird.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.563
I mean, "Dirty Jobs" has been on 20,

00:00:40.563 --> 00:00:42.270
"Deadliest Catch" has been on 19.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.207
- Wow
- Which I'm still doing.

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:49.560
So I think in this
industry, everybody's...

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.140
You look for hits and
you look for what's new.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.290
And the thing I was really
fortunate to stumble across

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.050
were shows that had big themes under them.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.670
So it's all kinda the same.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.650
It's just me tapping the country

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.327
on the shoulder every week
saying, "What about her?

00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:05.197
"What about him?

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.020
"Get a load of that."
- Which is so cool.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.880
- And people still
watch, it's unbelievable.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.220
- Yeah.
- I mean, I'm castrating cats

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.040
on international television
and people are watching.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.440
- Of course, we are.
- Why would we not?

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.400
- Why? Yeah.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.353
- I had a ball

00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:20.799
(laughing)

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:22.216
- You had a ball?

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.360
That was really good.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.530
Wait, have y'all ever met?

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.880
Zoe, have y'all ever met? Okay.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.250
- No. But I feel like I've
known you for like two minutes.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.083
- Two minutes.

00:01:31.143 --> 00:01:32.670
A solid five minutes.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.040
So wait, did you ever
get in a scary situation

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.217
with "Dirty Jobs" where you were like,

00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:37.950
"Ah, this is too far?"

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.950
- Only every day for 20 years.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.384
- Okay.

00:01:42.384 --> 00:01:43.920
- I mean, my job on that show,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.000
I mean, if you haven't seen it,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.740
is to essentially assume the
identity of an apprentice.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.980
So it's Groundhog's Day for me every day.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.780
It's the first day on the job.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.190
- What's like the scariest, though?

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.780
- Well, if you gotta thing for heights,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.540
you probably don't wanna be
on top of the Mackinac Bridge

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.956
or a radio tower changing light bulbs.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:03.789
- I don't have a thing
for... I don't mind heights,

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:05.130
but I still don't wanna do it.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.840
- If you have a thing for tight places,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.340
you probably don't wanna opal mine.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.410
You probably don't wanna be

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.540
in some of these crazy jammed up places,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.640
a mile below the earth's surface.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.227
- Nope.

00:02:16.227 --> 00:02:18.270
- So you never know what
you're gonna get on the show

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.980
except for an honest look at work.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.020
(audience laughing)

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.310
Why are they laughing? What happened?

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.143
Did something bad happen?

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:25.532
- Her face.
(laughing)

00:02:25.532 --> 00:02:26.449
- Oh, okay.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.410
- Crossing bridge.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.243
- No, but it's true.

00:02:29.243 --> 00:02:33.300
Zoe, so like you were talking
about jumping out of a plane.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.400
So I mean, that's a great example.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.740
I jumped out of a plane
with the Golden Knights

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.730
who were essentially the
sort of the Blue Angels

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:42.563
of the army.

00:02:42.563 --> 00:02:43.396
- I've done the Blue Angel thing, yeah.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:44.820
- Praying paratroopers.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.850
And yeah, the first time you
go out and pull your own shoot

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.280
is a moment that you're never, ever, ever,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.800
ever, ever, ever, ever gonna forget.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.888
- Or never, ever, ever, ever have.

00:02:55.888 --> 00:02:56.760
- Yeah.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.410
It makes an impression.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.570
But that's the the point of the show,

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.490
it's not to be an adrenaline junkie,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.407
it's just to show people,
"Look, these are the people

00:03:05.407 --> 00:03:07.087
"who are building our world.

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:08.887
"They're tending to our civilization.

00:03:08.887 --> 00:03:11.640
"This is what they do every single day."

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.110
Now I've done it for 20 years,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.450
but in the end I go home, I take a shower,

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.680
I come, I do your show,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.380
I sing "Deck The Halls"
and get on with my life.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.400
- But this is their every day.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.750
- This is the world we live in.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.110
And without these folks,
party's over. Right?

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.790
Plumbers, steam fitters, pipe fitters.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.230
- I think we all figured
that out during COVID

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.987
when nobody could come to the house.

00:03:29.987 --> 00:03:30.820
- Real quick.
- Yeah.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.220
- Well, that's why "Dirty Jobs" came back.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.250
Essential work suddenly
became headline news.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.770
And now everybody's talking
about the definition

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.560
of what it means to have an essential job,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.730
what it means to work.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.782
So, you know, not to overthink it.

00:03:43.782 --> 00:03:44.615
- And what we took for granted.

00:03:44.615 --> 00:03:46.590
- And what we take for granted every day.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.090
- Yeah. Agreed.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.770
Ugh, like indoor plumbing.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.720
I had to do a lot of things in
a forest I did not wanna do.

